course: dictionary
lesson: 12979
title: Exotic: faraway and unusual
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Exotic describes something from a faraway country, especially a hot one, that feels unusual and exciting. The clues in the sentence show you what it means. «We ate exotic fruit on holiday: mango, papaya and dragon fruit.» Those fruits are unusual and come from far away, so exotic means from a faraway place and unusual.
explain: Sometimes the clues point to a different meaning. «The shop window was full of exotic shells and bright feathers.» Nothing here mentions a faraway country, but the shells are very unusual and colourful, so exotic can also mean unusual and colourful, not ordinary.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping in a simpler word. Try «ordinary fruit» in place of «exotic fruit». The sentence now sounds dull, so exotic must mean something special and unusual.
explain: Scientists use exotic in a more exact way. «Grey squirrels are an exotic species in Britain, brought over from North America.» Here exotic means a plant or animal brought to a place where it does not naturally live.
fact: Exotic means coming from or belonging to a faraway country, especially a hot one.
fact: «Exotic» can also mean very unusual and colourful, not ordinary.
fact: In science, an exotic species is a plant or animal brought to a place where it does not naturally live.
fact: Exotic or invasive species, like rabbits in Australia or grey squirrels in Britain, can harm native wildlife.
fact: Be careful: calling a person «exotic» can sound unkind, as if they are strange and do not belong.
exercise: «We ate exotic fruit on holiday: mango, papaya and dragon fruit.» What does exotic mean here? | Ordinary and found in every shop | *From a faraway, hot country and unusual | Frozen solid | Sour and green
exercise: «The shop window was full of exotic shells and bright feathers.» Find and copy one word that shows the shells are colourful. | window | shop | *bright | full
exercise: In science, an exotic species is... What does exotic species mean? | A sweet fruit sold in a shop | *A plant or animal brought to a place where it does not naturally live | A kind of weather | A plant or animal that has always lived in that place
test: «The zoo has exotic pets, such as a snake from the rainforest.» What does exotic most likely mean? | *Unusual, from a faraway place | Lost | Sleepy and tired | Cheap and common
test: Why might calling a new friend «exotic» sound unkind? | It means they are very tall | It means they are hungry | *It can make them sound strange, as if they do not belong | It is a long, hard word
exam: Which sentence uses exotic as a science word? | *Grey squirrels are an exotic species in Britain, brought from North America. | Exotic places are hot. | The exotic mango was sweet. | Her exotic shells were bright.
